WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.462
Imagine a sorting program that reads one file,
produces its answer, and exits. Its running

00:00:05.462 --> 00:00:10.863
time can be studied as the input grows. Now
expose that calculation as a network service.

00:00:10.863 --> 00:00:16.204
The process remains available, accepts requests
from unrelated clients, and may still be

00:00:16.204 --> 00:00:18.875
working on one request when another arrives.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.395
The input size of an individual job is no longer
the only source of growth: the arrival rate

00:00:24.395 --> 00:00:29.555
and the overlap between jobs matter too. In the
diagram, several clients feed one worker

00:00:29.555 --> 00:00:34.950
pool. Increasing the number of clients without
increasing useful processing capacity creates

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.290
waiting, even if every individual calculation
uses an efficient algorithm.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:45.201
Consider a service that completes 80 small
lookups per second under a particular workload.

00:00:45.201 --> 00:00:50.649
If arrivals rise to 100 per second for ten
seconds, a simplified model accumulates 200

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:56.349
unfinished requests. When arrivals return to 60
per second, the service has 20 completions

00:00:56.349 --> 00:01:01.607
per second available to remove that backlog, so
recovery takes another ten seconds.

00:01:01.607 --> 00:01:06.968
The queue is a temporary reservoir, not an extra
processor. This example explains why a

00:01:06.968 --> 00:01:12.268
capacity claim must include more than registered
users or a peak request count. Follow

00:01:12.268 --> 00:01:17.937
arrivals, completions, waiting time, and errors
together; otherwise an apparently successful

00:01:17.937 --> 00:01:22.066
service may merely be postponing the work until
clients abandon it.

00:01:22.107 --> 00:01:27.399
Suppose a service spends most of its time
waiting for a file or a remote database. While
one

00:01:27.399 --> 00:01:32.461
request is blocked, another request can use the
processor. Several threads can therefore

00:01:32.461 --> 00:01:35.740
improve utilization even on a machine with only
one core.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:41.314
That does not mean the core executes several
computations at the same instant: the operating

00:01:41.314 --> 00:01:46.887
system schedules runnable work while blocked
work waits. In the illustration, request A uses

00:01:46.887 --> 00:01:52.279
the CPU, waits for storage, and resumes; request
B occupies part of A's waiting interval.

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:57.575
If the work instead consists almost entirely of
computation, overlapping threads cannot

00:01:57.575 --> 00:02:01.227
manufacture additional execution capacity on
that same core.

00:02:01.279 --> 00:02:06.986
Vertical scaling replaces or upgrades a machine
with more useful resources: cores, memory,

00:02:06.986 --> 00:02:12.629
storage bandwidth, or faster processors.
Horizontal scaling adds machines and distributes

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:18.336
requests between them. Picture two application
replicas that both query the same database.

00:02:18.336 --> 00:02:23.308
Adding the second replica helps only while that
database and the connecting network can

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:28.451
support the extra work. If the database already
limits completions, two application queues

00:02:28.451 --> 00:02:33.709
simply converge on the same narrow point. The
figure contrasts extra execution capacity with

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:35.481
an unchanged shared dependency.

00:02:35.481 --> 00:02:40.494
Diagnose the limiting resource first, change one
capacity assumption, and repeat the same

00:02:40.494 --> 00:02:44.775
workload measurement to establish whether the
bottleneck has actually moved.
